{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794529406322,"sessionID":"ses_e2db8016dffezkR2BwTBxjhrBS","part":{"id":"prt_1d249ad72001vi8MjHVXEC8Ypp","sessionID":"ses_e2db8016dffezkR2BwTBxjhrBS","messageID":"msg_1d249a759001ZbS72qwbUOLrsU","type":"step-start","snapshot":"816a62e2bd6ab419f969a04c4026c8cee88fd050"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794529407993,"sessionID":"ses_e2db8016dffezkR2BwTBxjhrBS","part":{"partID":"prt_1d249b1ec0019EbJ8u8kKAaEeD","sessionID":"ses_e2db8016dffezkR2BwTBxjhrBS","messageID":"msg_1d249a759001ZbS72qwbUOLrsU","type":"tool","id":"call_71ebc75ed6014dfd9ac19a13","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '1,120p' .opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"---\nname: sk-code-skilled\ndescription: \"sk-code SKILLED surface: system-code evidence (TypeScript/Python/shell/Rust/config standards, language-agnostic patterns, hooks, alignment verification, and skill/agent/command/MCP authoring checklists) plus shared implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine.\"\nallowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob]\nversion: 2.1.18.0\nmetadata:\n  family: sk-code\n  packetKind: surface\n---\n\n# Skilled Surface — System-Code Evidence\n\n**Domain evidence** and shared workflow doctrine for OpenCode system code (the `.skilled/` tree: skills, agents, commands, plugins, MCP servers, config, changelogs, and runtime bridge wiring). This surface owns the implement -> debug -> verify phases through the workflow references below, then slices evidence by the detected language so a TypeScript task never pulls the Python/shell/config guides.\n\nDetection has two steps:\n\n- First, select the Skilled surface for work under `.skilled/`. This includes `SKILL.md`, descriptors, commands, agents, plugins, MCP servers, assets, scripts and changelogs.\n- Then use file extensions and local markers to select the language trio:\n  - `.cjs`/`.mjs`/`.js` -> JavaScript\n  - `.ts`/`.tsx`/`.mts`/`.d.ts` -> TypeScript\n  - `.py` plus `argparse` -> Python\n  - `.sh`/`.bash` -> shell\n  - `.rs` -> Rust\n  - `.json`/`.jsonc`/`.yaml`/`.yml` plus `graph-metadata` or `spec-folder` -> config\n\nFor Rust, when no `.rs` file is present, local `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock` markers select it after the Skilled surface is established.\nnapi-rs and wasm-bindgen vocabulary are additional intent signals, not cross-project surface detectors.\n\n## 1. WHEN THE HUB BUNDLES THIS\n\n- The task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, or descriptor/config.\n- The active workflow phase needs a language standard, a language-agnostic organization pattern, a hook contract, an alignment-verification procedure, or an authoring checklist.\n- This surface owns edits, tests, and verification through the workflow references; hand off formal findings-first review to `sk-code-review` and author-side quality gates to `sk-code-quality`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nThe hub bundles this surface when the task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, descriptor or config — and a language or authoring signal then selects the leaf. The first leaves it loads are the shared tier under `references/shared/` and `references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md`, because every language standard builds on them.\n\nThe parent hub `RESOURCE_MAP` carries this map re-prefixed with `sk-code-skilled/` plus the parent-owned universal tier, and the router-sync guard (`assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs`, leg 2) checks that equality. A new doc under `references/` or `assets/` needs a `RESOURCE_MAP` entry here, or leg 1b fails.\n\nRead [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n\n---\n\n## 3. REFERENCE MAP\n\nLanguage standards — after `.skilled/` selects this surface, load the exact split resources for the detected language:\n- TypeScript:\n  - `references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md`\n  - `references/typescript/style-guide/formatting-imports-and-coexistence.md`\n  - `references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md`\n  - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsdoc-errors-and-async.md`\n  - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsconfig-and-modules.md`\n  - `references/typescript/quick-reference/template-naming-and-types.md`\n  - `references/typescript/quick-reference/imports-errors-and-tsconfig.md`\n- Python — `references/python/style-guide.md`, `references/python/quality-standards.md`, `references/python/quick-reference.md`\n- Shell:\n  - `references/shell/style-guide/overview-structure-and-naming.md`\n  - `references/shell/style-guide/variables-functions-and-output.md`\n  - `references/shell/quality-standards/overview-and-priority-blockers.md`\n  - `references/shell/quality-standards/validation-security-and-shellcheck.md`\n  - `references/shell/quick-reference/template-variables-and-loops.md`\n  - `references/shell/quick-reference/functions-strings-and-checklist.md`\n- Rust:\n  - Style guide:\n    - `references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md`\n    - `references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md`\n    - `references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md`\n    - `references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md`\n    - `references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md`\n    - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md`\n    - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md`\n  - Quality standards:\n    - `references/rust/quality-standards/overview-and-data-ownership.md`\n    - `references/rust/quality-standards/modeling-collections-and-api.md`\n    - `references/rust/quality-standards/docs-errors-and-async.md`\n    - `references/rust/quality-standards/build-and-organization.md`\n    - `references/rust/quality-standards/determinism-and-parity.md`\n  - Quick reference:\n    - `references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md`\n    - `references/rust/quick-reference/naming-ordering-and-signatures.md`\n    - `references/rust/quick-reference/collections-imports-and-errors.md`\n    - `references/rust/quick-reference/rustdoc-and-cargo.md`\n    - `references/rust/quick-reference/determinism-parity-and-related.md`\n- Config (JSON/JSONC/YAML descriptors and route assets) — `references/config/style-guide.md`, `references/config/quality-standards.md`, `references/config/quick-reference.md`, `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md`\n- JavaScript (CommonJS/ESM plugins) — `references/javascript/style-guide.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md`, `references/javascript/quick-reference.md`\n\nLanguage-agnostic shared tier (`references/shared/`, always kept within OpenCode regardless of language):\n- `references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`, `references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md`\n- `hooks.md` — runtime hook entrypoints, checked-in Claude wiring, OpenCode plugin-bridge delivery, and wrapper reachability; defer to that file for current hook infrastructure instead of duplicating it here\n- `alignment-verification-automation.md` — the alignment-drift verifier\n- `workflow-guardrails.md`: OpenCode-only implementation guardrails, the verification command chain and runtime build traps, applied on top of the shared implement and verify workflow\n\nAuthoring and validation assets:\n\n- Checklists:\n  - `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/python-checklist.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md`\n- Scripts:\n  - `assets/scripts/README.md`\n- References:\n  - `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`\n  - `references/shared/hooks.md`\n\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: sk-code-skilled\ndescription: \"sk-code SKILLED surface: system-code evidence (TypeScript/Python/shell/Rust/config standards, language-agnostic patterns, hooks, alignment verification, and skill/agent/command/MCP authoring checklists) plus shared implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine.\"\nallowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob]\nversion: 2.1.18.0\nmetadata:\n  family: sk-code\n  packetKind: surface\n---\n\n# Skilled Surface — System-Code Evidence\n\n**Domain evidence** and shared workflow doctrine for OpenCode system code (the `.skilled/` tree: skills, agents, commands, plugins, MCP servers, config, changelogs, and runtime bridge wiring). This surface owns the implement -> debug -> verify phases through the workflow references below, then slices evidence by the detected language so a TypeScript task never pulls the Python/shell/config guides.\n\nDetection has two steps:\n\n- First, select the Skilled surface for work under `.skilled/`. This includes `SKILL.md`, descriptors, commands, agents, plugins, MCP servers, assets, scripts and changelogs.\n- Then use file extensions and local markers to select the language trio:\n  - `.cjs`/`.mjs`/`.js` -> JavaScript\n  - `.ts`/`.tsx`/`.mts`/`.d.ts` -> TypeScript\n  - `.py` plus `argparse` -> Python\n  - `.sh`/`.bash` -> shell\n  - `.rs` -> Rust\n  - `.json`/`.jsonc`/`.yaml`/`.yml` plus `graph-metadata` or `spec-folder` -> config\n\nFor Rust, when no `.rs` file is present, local `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock` markers select it after the Skilled surface is established.\nnapi-rs and wasm-bindgen vocabulary are additional intent signals, not cross-project surface detectors.\n\n## 1. WHEN THE HUB BUNDLES THIS\n\n- The task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, or descriptor/config.\n- The active workflow phase needs a language standard, a language-agnostic organization pattern, a hook contract, an alignment-verification procedure, or an authoring checklist.\n- This surface owns edits, tests, and verification through the workflow references; hand off formal findings-first review to `sk-code-review` and author-side quality gates to `sk-code-quality`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nThe hub bundles this surface when the task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, descriptor or config — and a language or authoring signal then selects the leaf. The first leaves it loads are the shared tier under `references/shared/` and `references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md`, because every language standard builds on them.\n\nThe parent hub `RESOURCE_MAP` carries this map re-prefixed with `sk-code-skilled/` plus the parent-owned universal tier, and the router-sync guard (`assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs`, leg 2) checks that equality. A new doc under `references/` or `assets/` needs a `RESOURCE_MAP` entry here, or leg 1b fails.\n\nRead [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n\n---\n\n## 3. REFERENCE MAP\n\nLanguage standards — after `.skilled/` selects this surface, load the exact split resources for the detected language:\n- TypeScript:\n  - `references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md`\n  - `references/typescript/style-guide/formatting-imports-and-coexistence.md`\n  - `references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md`\n  - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsdoc-errors-and-async.md`\n  - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsconfig-and-modules.md`\n  - `references/typescript/quick-reference/template-naming-and-types.md`\n  - `references/typescript/quick-reference/imports-errors-and-tsconfig.md`\n- Python — `references/python/style-guide.md`, `references/python/quality-standards.md`, `references/python/quick-reference.md`\n- Shell:\n  - `references/shell/style-guide/overview-structure-and-naming.md`\n  - `references/shell/style-guide/variables-functions-and-output.md`\n  - `references/shell/quality-standards/overview-and-priority-blockers.md`\n  - `references/shell/quality-standards/validation-security-and-shellcheck.md`\n  - `references/shell/quick-reference/template-variables-and-loops.md`\n  - `references/shell/quick-reference/functions-strings-and-checklist.md`\n- Rust:\n  - Style guide:\n    - `references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md`\n    - `references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md`\n    - `references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md`\n    - `references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md`\n    - `references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md`\n    - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md`\n    - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md`\n  - Quality standards:\n    - `references/rust/quality-standards/overview-and-data-ownership.md`\n    - `references/rust/quality-standards/modeling-collections-and-api.md`\n    - `references/rust/quality-standards/docs-errors-and-async.md`\n    - `references/rust/quality-standards/build-and-organization.md`\n    - `references/rust/quality-standards/determinism-and-parity.md`\n  - Quick reference:\n    - `references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md`\n    - `references/rust/quick-reference/naming-ordering-and-signatures.md`\n    - `references/rust/quick-reference/collections-imports-and-errors.md`\n    - `references/rust/quick-reference/rustdoc-and-cargo.md`\n    - `references/rust/quick-reference/determinism-parity-and-related.md`\n- Config (JSON/JSONC/YAML descriptors and route assets) — `references/config/style-guide.md`, `references/config/quality-standards.md`, `references/config/quick-reference.md`, `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md`\n- JavaScript (CommonJS/ESM plugins) — `references/javascript/style-guide.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md`, `references/javascript/quick-reference.md`\n\nLanguage-agnostic shared tier (`references/shared/`, always kept within OpenCode regardless of language):\n- `references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`, `references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md`\n- `hooks.md` — runtime hook entrypoints, checked-in Claude wiring, OpenCode plugin-bridge delivery, and wrapper reachability; defer to that file for current hook infrastructure instead of duplicating it here\n- `alignment-verification-automation.md` — the alignment-drift verifier\n- `workflow-guardrails.md`: OpenCode-only implementation guardrails, the verification command chain and runtime build traps, applied on top of the shared implement and verify workflow\n\nAuthoring and validation assets:\n\n- Checklists:\n  - `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/python-checklist.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md`\n  - `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md`\n- Scripts:\n  - `assets/scripts/README.md`\n- References:\n  - `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`\n  - `references/shared/hooks.md`\n\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"sed -n '1,120p' .opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research\"}"},"time":{"start":1794529407468,"end":1794529407993}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794529409864,"sessionID":"ses_e2db8016dffezkR2BwTBxjhrBS","part":{"id":"prt_1d249bb4800178WX1aTZm4NwMx","sessionID":"ses_e2db8016dffezkR2BwTBxjhrBS","messageID":"msg_1d249a759001ZbS72qwbUOLrsU","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"5078b18f2822597bddeed099937f8b6db792ef13","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":346,"output":101,"reasoning":104,"cache":{"read":60288,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794529411996,"sessionID":"ses_e2db8016dffezkR2BwTBxjhrBS","part":{"id":"prt_1d249c39c001iL4rDpstOGepLx","sessionID":"ses_e2db8016dffezkR2BwTBxjhrBS","messageID":"msg_1d249bb4e001RT3I7sZrfUO77m","type":"step-start","snapshot":"bdc7f0f73e6e674d8f5f79f6eb17fe58da58acc6"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794529412349,"sessionID":"ses_e2db8016dffezkR2BwTBxjhrBS","part":{"partID":"prt_1d249c39d001OAXNvArORygAjK","sessionID":"ses_e2db8016dffezkR2BwTBxjhrBS","messageID":"msg_1d249bb4e001RT3I7sZrfUO77m","type":"tool","id":"call_ff6e7f7f5d10405d9dc2fde1","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cat .opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/style-guide.md 2>/dev/null | head -80; echo \"====QUALITY====\"; cat .opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md 2>/dev/null | head -80","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"---\ntitle: JavaScript Style Guide\ndescription: Formatting standards and naming conventions for JavaScript files in the OpenCode development environment.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"opencode javascript style guide\"\n  - \"javascript file header format\"\n  - \"use strict directive\"\n  - \"javascript section organization\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.0.4.0\n---\n\n# JavaScript Style Guide\n\nOpen this when you are drafting or reviewing a JavaScript file and need the header, section order, naming rule or formatting detail that settles the point.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nDefines consistent styling rules for JavaScript files to ensure readability, maintainability, and alignment across all OpenCode JavaScript code.\n\n### When to Use\n\nUse when writing, reviewing, or resolving style questions for JavaScript files.\n\n### Use Cases\n\n- Writing new JavaScript files\n- Reviewing JavaScript code for consistency\n- Resolving style disagreements in code review\n\n---\n\n## 2. FILE HEADER FORMAT\n\nAll JavaScript files MUST begin with a module header identifying the module,\nusing the divider form below. Module syntax follows the nearest package\nboundary and loader contract: `.cjs` is CommonJS, `.mjs` is ESM, and `.js`\nfollows the nearest package's `type`. OpenCode plugin paths use their ESM\nloader contract.\n\n### Template\n\n```javascript\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: [Module Name]\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n```\n\n### Requirements\n\n- Divider width: 67 box-drawing characters after `// `\n- Module name: after `MODULE:` on the middle line\n- A shebang line may precede the header in an executable script\n- Immediately followed by `'use strict';` for `.js/.cjs` files, as required by\n  this repository's alignment checks; it is redundant in ESM `.js` files\n- `.mjs` files do not require `'use strict'`; ESM `.js` plugin files follow the\n  `.js` requirement above\n- The older 78-character `╔═╗` box is accepted where it already exists; do not\n  introduce it in new files\n\n**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/dist-freshness.cjs:1-4`,\n`.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/generate-trigger-index.mjs:1-4`\n\n---\n\n## 3. USE STRICT DIRECTIVE\n\nEvery `.js` and `.cjs` JavaScript file MUST include the strict mode directive.\nThis repository convention also applies to ESM `.js` files, where it is\nredundant because modules are strict by definition. Do not add it to `.mjs`\nfiles; the alignment verifier intentionally skips `.mjs` enforcement.\n\n```javascript\n'use strict';\n```\n====QUALITY====\n---\ntitle: JavaScript Quality Standards\ndescription: Module organization, error handling, documentation, and security patterns for JavaScript files.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"opencode javascript quality standards\"\n  - \"javascript module organization\"\n  - \"commonjs export pattern\"\n  - \"javascript error handling standards\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.0.3.0\n---\n\n# JavaScript Quality Standards\n\nReach for this file when a JavaScript module boundary, error path or security rule needs a decision that survives review.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nEstablishes module organization, error handling, documentation, and security patterns that all JavaScript code must follow.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Writing new JavaScript modules\n- Reviewing code for quality compliance\n- Setting up error handling and security patterns\n\n---\n\n## 2. MODULE ORGANIZATION\n\n### Module-System Boundary\n\nUse the module format required by the file's package boundary and loader\ncontract. Do not force one JavaScript module style across all paths.\n\n| Surface | Module Pattern | Notes |\n|---------|----------------|-------|\n| `.js` files | Follow the nearest package `type` and loader contract | `\"type\": \"module\"` selects ESM; otherwise use the package's CommonJS boundary |\n| `.cjs` files | CommonJS | The extension selects CommonJS |\n| `.mjs` files | ESM | The verifier skips `'use strict'` enforcement for `.mjs` |\n| `.opencode/plugins/*.{js,mjs,ts}` | ESM default export | Required by the OpenCode plugin loader |\n\n### CommonJS Pattern\n\n- Use CommonJS for `.cjs` files and `.js` files whose package boundary selects CommonJS.\n- Include the repository's required `'use strict'` directive in those files.\n- ESM `.js` files follow their package boundary and also keep that directive to satisfy the repository's alignment checks; it is redundant in ESM.\n\n```javascript\n'use strict';\n\n// ... imports and implementation ...\n\nmodule.exports = {\n  // Exports here\n};\n```\n\n**Evidence**: Current TypeScript packages are ESM/NodeNext; this CommonJS\npattern applies to legacy `.js/.cjs` utility surfaces and test runners that use\n`require`.\n\n### Export Pattern\n\n- Export functions using `camelCase` names.\n- For MCP handlers, include `snake_case` aliases for backward compatibility.\n- Do not apply this export shape to OpenCode plugin paths; those use `export default`.\n\n```javascript\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 5. EXPORTS\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nmodule.exports = {\n  // Primary exports (camelCase)\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\ntitle: JavaScript Style Guide\ndescription: Formatting standards and naming conventions for JavaScript files in the OpenCode development environment.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"opencode javascript style guide\"\n  - \"javascript file header format\"\n  - \"use strict directive\"\n  - \"javascript section organization\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.0.4.0\n---\n\n# JavaScript Style Guide\n\nOpen this when you are drafting or reviewing a JavaScript file and need the header, section order, naming rule or formatting detail that settles the point.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nDefines consistent styling rules for JavaScript files to ensure readability, maintainability, and alignment across all OpenCode JavaScript code.\n\n### When to Use\n\nUse when writing, reviewing, or resolving style questions for JavaScript files.\n\n### Use Cases\n\n- Writing new JavaScript files\n- Reviewing JavaScript code for consistency\n- Resolving style disagreements in code review\n\n---\n\n## 2. FILE HEADER FORMAT\n\nAll JavaScript files MUST begin with a module header identifying the module,\nusing the divider form below. Module syntax follows the nearest package\nboundary and loader contract: `.cjs` is CommonJS, `.mjs` is ESM, and `.js`\nfollows the nearest package's `type`. OpenCode plugin paths use their ESM\nloader contract.\n\n### Template\n\n```javascript\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: [Module Name]\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n```\n\n### Requirements\n\n- Divider width: 67 box-drawing characters after `// `\n- Module name: after `MODULE:` on the middle line\n- A shebang line may precede the header in an executable script\n- Immediately followed by `'use strict';` for `.js/.cjs` files, as required by\n  this repository's alignment checks; it is redundant in ESM `.js` files\n- `.mjs` files do not require `'use strict'`; ESM `.js` plugin files follow the\n  `.js` requirement above\n- The older 78-character `╔═╗` box is accepted where it already exists; do not\n  introduce it in new files\n\n**Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/dist-freshness.cjs:1-4`,\n`.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/generate-trigger-index.mjs:1-4`\n\n---\n\n## 3. USE STRICT DIRECTIVE\n\nEvery `.js` and `.cjs` JavaScript file MUST include the strict mode directive.\nThis repository convention also applies to ESM `.js` files, where it is\nredundant because modules are strict by definition. Do not add it to `.mjs`\nfiles; the alignment verifier intentionally skips `.mjs` enforcement.\n\n```javascript\n'use strict';\n```\n====QUALITY====\n---\ntitle: JavaScript Quality Standards\ndescription: Module organization, error handling, documentation, and security patterns for JavaScript files.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"opencode javascript quality standards\"\n  - \"javascript module organization\"\n  - \"commonjs export pattern\"\n  - \"javascript error handling standards\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.0.3.0\n---\n\n# JavaScript Quality Standards\n\nReach for this file when a JavaScript module boundary, error path or security rule needs a decision that survives review.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nEstablishes module organization, error handling, documentation, and security patterns that all JavaScript code must follow.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Writing new JavaScript modules\n- Reviewing code for quality compliance\n- Setting up error handling and security patterns\n\n---\n\n## 2. MODULE ORGANIZATION\n\n### Module-System Boundary\n\nUse the module format required by the file's package boundary and loader\ncontract. Do not force one JavaScript module style across all paths.\n\n| Surface | Module Pattern | Notes |\n|---------|----------------|-------|\n| `.js` files | Follow the nearest package `type` and loader contract | `\"type\": \"module\"` selects ESM; otherwise use the package's CommonJS boundary |\n| `.cjs` files | CommonJS | The extension selects CommonJS |\n| `.mjs` files | ESM | The verifier skips `'use strict'` enforcement for `.mjs` |\n| `.opencode/plugins/*.{js,mjs,ts}` | ESM default export | Required by the OpenCode plugin loader |\n\n### CommonJS Pattern\n\n- Use CommonJS for `.cjs` files and `.js` files whose package boundary selects CommonJS.\n- Include the repository's required `'use strict'` directive in those files.\n- ESM `.js` files follow their package boundary and also keep that directive to satisfy the repository's alignment checks; it is redundant in ESM.\n\n```javascript\n'use strict';\n\n// ... imports and implementation ...\n\nmodule.exports = {\n  // Exports here\n};\n```\n\n**Evidence**: Current TypeScript packages are ESM/NodeNext; this CommonJS\npattern applies to legacy `.js/.cjs` utility surfaces and test runners that use\n`require`.\n\n### Export Pattern\n\n- Export functions using `camelCase` names.\n- For MCP handlers, include `snake_case` aliases for backward compatibility.\n- Do not apply this export shape to OpenCode plugin paths; those use `export default`.\n\n```javascript\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 5. EXPORTS\n// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nmodule.exports = {\n  // Primary exports (camelCase)\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cat .opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/style-guide.md 2>/dev/null | head -80; echo \\\"====QUALITY====\\\"; cat .opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md 2>/dev/null | head -80\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research\"}"},"time":{"start":1794529411997,"end":1794529412349}}}}
